  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ecosystems Approach for Monitoring Biodiversity and Conservation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Recipients goal is to enhance the capacity for monitoring the biodiversity of small mammals and conserving endangered and protected species such as the black-footed ferret and black-tailed prairie dogs by training local wildlife managers in biodiversity monitoring in several reserves in Chihuahua, Mexico. Through this grant the Recipient will : 1) train 35 local biologists and park rangers in small mammal biodiversity assessment techniques (assessment and trapping methods, use of identification keys , biopsies for genetic bar-coding in cryptic species, necropsy procedures, and bio-safety principles for handling wildlife); 2) train 6 natural protected areas managers in the importance of ecosystem health, biodiversity and the conservation of endangered species; and 3) develop a biodiversity assessment program. Project products include: purchase of a -20C freezer for bio-banking biological samples to be housed and used in the Janos-Casas Grandes Biosphere Reserve, together with a user manual; produce an user guide on biodiversity assessment techniques; distribute learning materials on small mammal capture and sampling techniques, a necropsy guide and rodent identification guides; develop an information management system for data entry; develop a written program for wildlife biodiversity surveillance; distribute 30 small mammal traps; sign cooperative agreements or MOUs between the national diagnostic or university laboratories and the participant reserves to analyze the samples collected; and publish a scientific article in a peer-reviewed journal.</narrative>
  </description>
